# Manifest discipline

**All combat content** — dice, leans, gilded faces, jokers, spells, skills,
enemy rules, economy toggles — lives in **`grammar.yaml`** plus experiment
overlays. Nothing else holds any of it.

**Engine code never names a specific die or joker.** Not in a constant, not in
a switch, not in a comment that a later reader will treat as a list. The engine
knows hooks and effect verbs; the manifest attaches verbs to hooks.

The test is simple: **adding or removing a die must be a data edit.** If it
takes an engine change, the abstraction has already failed.

```
grammar.yaml            the live set — tiers, faces, dice, jokers, spells,
                        skills, enemy_rules, and the `enabled` list
experiments/exp-NNN.yaml  base overrides ONLY — enable/disable lists and
                        numeric tweaks. Nothing else changes anywhere.
```

## the two kinds of change

- **A new die, joker, face, spell, skill or enemy rule** is a `grammar.yaml`
  edit plus tests. Ordinary work.
- **A new effect verb or a new pipeline hook** is an **engine amendment card** —
  rare, loud, and argued. It widens a closed language
  (`.llm/rules/two-languages.mdc`).

This is the same law the room engine already lives under, applied to combat.
